## The brief that made it to filing

A friend who runs IT for a mid-size firm told me about the week his legal team almost lost a partner. Associate drafts a motion, uses the in-house chatbot to "find supporting case law," gets back three citations with case names, court, year, and a one-sentence summary of each. They look right. They read right. They get pasted into the brief.

Two of the three cases do not exist. The third exists but says the opposite of what the summary claims. The opposing counsel's paralegal catches it in twenty minutes.

What everyone wanted to know — partners, associates, the IT director who got the angry phone call — was the same question: *how does a tool this good produce something this bad without flinching?* No error message. No hedge. No "I'm not sure." Just three fluent, plausible, completely fabricated citations sitting next to two real ones.

## The hand-up metaphor

If you read the earlier piece on [LLMs as a loose database](/demystify), you already have most of the picture. The model is not looking anything up. It is generating each next word based on what *patterns of words like this one* tend to do. Citations are a pattern. "Smith v. Jones, 412 F.3d 891 (9th Cir. 2005)" is a *shape* — plaintiff v. defendant, volume number, reporter, court, year. The model has seen tens of thousands of these. It knows the shape cold.

So when you ask for case law on a topic, the model does what it always does: it produces text that fits the shape of the answer. Plausible plaintiff. Plausible volume number. Plausible court for the jurisdiction. Plausible year. Plausible one-line summary in the register of legal headnotes.

```
Your prompt:  "cases on negligent retention in California"
                          │
                          ▼
            ┌─────────────────────────────┐
            │  Pattern: legal citation    │
            │  Pattern: CA jurisdiction   │
            │  Pattern: negligence verb   │
            └─────────────┬───────────────┘
                          ▼
              "Garcia v. Pacific Logistics,
               203 Cal.App.4th 411 (2012)"
                          │
              shape: ✓   existence: ?
```

The shape is correct. Whether the case actually exists is a separate question the model never asked.

## The small refinement

The refinement is this: there is no separate "fact-check" step inside the model. There is only one mechanism, generating one token at a time, and that mechanism does not distinguish between *recalling* and *confabulating*. From the model's perspective, both feel the same — they are both just "the next plausible token."

When the training data contained the real Smith v. Jones case thousands of times, the model's pattern-pull leans hard toward the real volume number and year. When it didn't — when you asked about a niche jurisdiction or an obscure topic — the pattern-pull is weaker, but the *shape generator still runs*. It will fill in a volume number that fits. It will fill in a year that fits. The output looks identical either way.

This is the part most people miss. Hallucination isn't the model "making things up" as a separate behavior. It's the model doing exactly what it always does, in a region of the space where the training data was thin.

## Why the imperfection is the feature

Here is the pedagogically important move, and it is the thing to actually take home: *the same mechanism that makes the model useful is the mechanism that makes it hallucinate.*

The looseness is what lets the model rephrase your messy prompt, summarize a document it has never seen before, generalize from "how to write a Python decorator" to "how to write a TypeScript decorator." If you bolted a strict "only emit tokens you can verify against ground truth" guardrail onto the model, you would not get a more honest assistant. You would get a much, much worse one — one that couldn't paraphrase, couldn't generalize, couldn't help you with anything novel.

Hallucination and helpfulness come out of the same pipe. You don't get to keep one and lose the other by tuning a knob. This is why "just make it stop hallucinating" is not a roadmap item — it's a category error.

## One layer deeper

There's one mechanical detail worth knowing. When the model is generating, at each step it is sampling from a probability distribution over possible next tokens. In a region with strong training support, that distribution is sharply peaked — one or two tokens are wildly more likely than the rest, and the model picks one of those. In a region with weak training support, the distribution is *flat* — many tokens are roughly equally likely, and the model picks one anyway, because there is no "abstain" token in the vocabulary.

The model has no internal signal that says "I'm in a flat-distribution region right now, you should not trust this." It just emits the token. Modern systems try to estimate this externally — confidence scoring, retrieval-augmented generation, tool-use to ground specific claims — but none of those are *the model itself* knowing it's guessing. They are scaffolding bolted on around it.

This is why fluency is such a poor signal for accuracy. A confident, well-formed sentence costs the model the same as a hesitant one. There is no internal cringe.

## How it goes wrong, and how to spot it

Two failure modes show up over and over.

**The plausible-shape fabrication.** Citations, API method names, RFC numbers, library functions, statistics, historical dates. Anything with a recognizable structure where the *shape* is well-trained but the *specific instance* may not be. Spot it by: any time the model produces a precise identifier (number, name, URL, citation), treat it as a hypothesis until you've checked it in a system of record.

**The confidently wrong synthesis.** The model takes two real things and connects them in a way that sounds reasonable but isn't true. "Drug X is contraindicated with drug Y" — both drugs real, contraindication invented. Spot it by: when the model is reasoning *across* facts rather than reciting them, the join is the weak point, not the endpoints.

## Takeaways

1. **Treat any specific identifier as a hypothesis.** Names, numbers, URLs, citations, version strings, function signatures — verify before you ship.
2. **Fluency is not a confidence signal.** The model sounds equally sure when recalling and when confabulating. Read style as style only.
3. **Topic obscurity raises hallucination rate.** If the answer requires niche knowledge, assume thinner training support and verify harder.
4. **Cross-claim joins are the weakest point.** When the model reasons across two facts, the connection is more likely to be invented than the facts themselves.
5. **Don't ask the model "are you sure?"** It will produce the shape of a confidence answer, with no more grounding than the original. Verify externally, against a source of truth.

## Read more

- *Lin, Hilton, Evans — "TruthfulQA: Measuring How Models Mimic Human Falsehoods"* (ACL 2022). The rigorous reference: a benchmark designed specifically around the failure mode this piece describes, with the data to show why scaling alone doesn't fix it.
- *Simon Willison — "Hallucinations in code are the least dangerous form"* (blog, March 2025). The accessible explainer: a working developer's framing of why some hallucination domains have natural verifiers and others don't, with practical implications for where to deploy LLMs.

